le-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Hall</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><source>Global impact, local action: new environmental policy in Latin America</source><year>2005</year><publisher-name>University of London, Institute for the Study of the Americas</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London, UK</publisher-loc><fpage>81</fpage><lpage>125</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Roberts</surname><given-names>J.T</given-names></name><name><surname>Thanos</surname><given-names>N.D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Trouble in paradise: globalization and environmental crises in Latin America</article-title><year>2003</year><publisher-name>Routledge</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London, UK</publisher-loc></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rodrigues</surname><given-names>M.G.M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Global environmentalism and local politics: transnational advocacy networks in Brazil, Ecuador, and India</article-title><year>2004</year><publisher-name>State University of New York Press</publisher-name><publisher-loc>Albany, NY</publisher-loc></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Santilli</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Moutinho</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Schwartzman</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Nepstad</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Curran</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Nobre</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Tropical deforestation and the Kyoto protocol</article-title><source>Clim. Change</source><volume>71</volume><year>2005</year><fpage>267</fpage><lpage>276</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1007/s10584-005-8074-6">doi:10.1007/s10584-005-8074-6</ext-link></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Santos</surname><given-names>B.A.D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Amazonia: Potencial Mineral e Perspectivas de Desenvolvimento</article-title><year>1981</year><publisher-name>T. A. Queiroz</publisher-name><publisher-loc>S&#x000e3;o Paulo, Brazil</publisher-loc></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schmink</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Wood</surname><given-names>C.H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Contested frontiers in Amazonia</article-title><year>1993</year><publisher-name>University of Florida Press</publisher-name><publisher-loc>Gainesville, FL</publisher-loc></citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Soares-Filho</surname><given-names>B.S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Modelling conservation in the Amazon Basin</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>440</volume><year>2006</year><fpage>520</fpage><lpage>523</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1038/nature04389">doi:10.1038/nature04389</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16554817</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Viola</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>O Movimento Ecol&#x000f3;gico no Brasil: do Ambientalismo &#x000e0; Ecopol&#x000ed;tica</article-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Padua</surname><given-names>J.A.D</given-names></name></person-group><source>Ecologia e Pol&#x000ed;tica no Brasil</source><year>1987</year><publisher-name>Espa&#x000e7;o e Tempo</publisher-name><publisher-loc>Rio de Janeiro, Brazil</publisher-loc><fpage>63</fpage><lpage>110</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Woodword</surname><given-names>H.D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Amazon alliance and the PMACI: possibililities for Indian participation in Brazilian planned development</article-title><source>Latin Am. Anthropol. Rev</source><volume>1</volume><year>1989</year><fpage>24</fpage><lpage>25</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1525/jlat.1989.1.1.24">doi:10.1525/jlat.1989.1.1.24</ext-link></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><title>Endnotes</title><fn id="fn1"><label>1</label><p>Within Brazil, groups that support both environmentalism and indigenous and popular movements causes are known as socio-environmentalists.</p></fn><fn id="fn2"><label>2</label><p>Just 5 days after the <italic>Science</italic> article was published, Brazil's Secretary of Planning and Strategic Investments, Jos&#x000e9; Paulo Silveira, held a press conference to state that <italic>Avan&#x000e7;a Brasil</italic> did not include 1&#x0200a;km of new roads and the waterways were chosen to be developed for their lesser impact (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib25">Ministerio de Planejamento 2001<italic>a</italic></xref>). Silveira said, &#x02018;The predictions of deforestation are exaggerated and do not reflect a more careful analysis of future scenarios, rather, they are based on data from 20 years ago before Brazil had environmental laws&#x02019;.</p></fn><fn id="fn3"><label>3</label><p>For example, in October 2006, Marina Silva and Celso Amorim, Ministers of Environment and International Relations, respectively, in an open editorial in the Folha de S&#x000e3;o Paulo, vehemently denounced any plans to &#x02018;internationalize the Amazon&#x02019; coming from western countries (in this case, the UK). Folha de Sao Paulo, &#x02018;A Amaz&#x000f4;nia n&#x000e3;o est&#x000e1; a venda&#x02019;, 17 October 2006.</p></fn></fn-group><fn-group><fn><p>One contribution of 27 to a Theme Issue &#x02018;Climate change and the fate of the Amazon&#x02019;.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>